P(X = k) = \binom{n}{k} p^k (1-p)^{n-k}
\] where \(n = 2\), \(k = 1\), and \(p = \frac{2}{5}\). Plugging in these values, we get: \[
P(X = 1) = \binom{2}{1} \left(\frac{2}{5}\right)^1 \left(\frac{3}{5}\right)^1 = 2 \cdot \frac{2}{5} \cdot \frac{3}{5} = \frac{12}{25}
\] </think> <answer> $\frac{12}{25}$ </answer>

